 - [ ] Network transparency dashboard (per-app, plain-language) — reuses PHANTOM's monitoring core. **Blocked on input, not effort:** PHANTOM's source isn't in this repo or this conversation — reusing its actual monitoring core means either Hamza sharing that code, or reimplementing something PHANTOM-*like* from scratch, which risks quietly diverging from what "reuse" was meant to mean here.
 - [x] Backup / Recovery **code complete 2026-08-30** — `darkos-backup.py`. Plain file-based backup (Python `tarfile`, not Btrfs snapshots — this sandbox's filesystem is ext2/ext3 so a snapshot approach couldn't be verified here regardless, and the spec didn't call for one specifically). A manifest at `~/.local/share/darkos/backups/manifest.json` tracks what was backed up, when, and how big it was. **Runtime-verified end to end:** backed up a real test folder through the actual UI, confirmed the resulting `.tar.gz` has correct internal paths (`tar -tzf`), then restored it and diffed the restored file against the original — byte-identical. `tarfile.extractall(..., filter="data")` (PEP 706) same as File Explorer's archive handling, guarding against path-traversal from an archive of unknown origin.
 - [x] Dashboard (Performance, Overlay); [x] Mission/Spaces — **both done 2026-08-30.** `darkos-dashboard.py`: live CPU% (computed from two `/proc/stat` samples a tick apart, the standard technique), memory, disk, and top-processes-by-memory, refreshing every 2 seconds. **Runtime-verified with an actual load test** — ran a real CPU-bound busy-loop in the background and confirmed the dashboard's CPU reading actually moved from 0% to 100% between ticks, and that the busy-loop's own process appeared in the live top-processes list. `darkos-mission.py`: real `hyprctl -j workspaces` / `hyprctl -j clients` calls, grouped by workspace, with real `hyprctl dispatch` actions to switch workspace or focus a window. **Correction from earlier in this same session:** this was first bucketed with Shield as "can't verify here" — that was too pessimistic. Shield's correctness is fundamentally unverifiable without a real scan engine; this is a data-display problem against hyprctl's documented, stable JSON schema, which is a fundamentally different and much more tractable category — same shape as Network Center's nmcli/bluetoothctl calls. Verified two separate things, both for real: (1) the graceful-failure path, which is genuinely real in this sandbox (`hyprctl is not installed`, confirmed via screenshot), and (2) the parsing/rendering logic, via a schema-accurate fake `hyprctl` script on `PATH` returning realistic workspace/client JSON (documented format, not invented) — confirmed correct grouping including an empty-workspace case, correct unicode title decoding, and confirmed the dispatch buttons don't crash the app when clicked. What's still unverified is purely cosmetic/compositor-specific (does it look right against real windows) — the logic itself has been checked both ways it can be.
